<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Erivan Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1828 CE · capital Erivan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Persian-ruled khanate around Yerevan and the Ararat plain, whose conquest by Russia in 1827-1828 began modern Armenia's territorial history.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Erivan</a> province had been an Iranian administrative unit since Safavid times. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747, its governors acted as autonomous khans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Ararat</a> plain and the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>, while recognising Persian overlordship whenever a strong shah ruled.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans ruled from the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Erivan</a> over a mixed population of Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etchmiadzin-cathedral">Etchmiadzin</a>, seat of the Armenian Catholicos, within the khanate. Irrigated farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and fruit sustained it. The khanate paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli-kakheti">Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti</a> before <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran</a> restored control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosein-qoli-khan-qajar">Hosein Qoli Khan Qajar</a>, governor from 1807, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardar's</a> Palace.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Russian troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Erivan</a> in 1827, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> in 1828 ceded the khanate to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. Combined with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a>, it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-oblast">Armenian Oblast</a>, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> settled there.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/erivan-khanate.jpg" alt="Erivan Khanate" width="320" height="187"><figcaption>Image: HistoryofIran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Erivan_Khanate_in_1820.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1828 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erivan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hosein Qoli Khan Qajar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam; Armenian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian; Azerbaijani; Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Armenia, Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Erivan, Iravan Khanate, Yerevan Khanate</dd></dl></aside></div>
